[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Wakigawa et al., 2024-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9f1a70d1dd46868ac8229668227f0e49c6d52fa6)
- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 8
[method]
The paper used mitoribosome profiling methods named MitoIP-Ribo-Seq, MitoIP-Thor-Ribo-Seq, and MitoIP-Thor-Disome-Seq. The described workflow included lysate preparation, mitoribosome isolation, sucrose density gradient fractionation, library preparation, sequencing, and data analysis mapping protected fragments to mitochondrial transcripts. MitoIP-Thor-Ribo-Seq and MitoIP-Thor-Disome-Seq were presented as variants for profiling mitoribosome occupancy and disome/collision signals, respectively. Standard Ribo-Seq and RNA-Seq were also included in the methods section. This is directly related to the proposed HEK293 mitoribosome profiling plan because both use codon-level ribosome footprint mapping to detect translation arrest, codon-specific elongation defects, and initiation-site usage, although the paper excerpt does not describe antibiotic treatments.
[results]
The provided excerpt contains only section headings and does not report numerical read counts, fold changes, p-values, footprint-length distributions, or statistical comparisons. The headings indicate qualitative outcomes: t6A modification primarily affected Lys codon elongation; f5C34 modification facilitated mitoribosome traversal of AUA codons; tau-m5U34 influenced UUG, UGG, and AAG codons; s2U decorations supported GAG translation and suppressed cryptic GAC misreading; queuosine modification modulated mitoribosome traversal; a MELAS-associated mitochondrial tRNA mutation caused mitoribosome queuing; widespread cryptic translation initiation sites were detected; and mitoribosome collision/disome signals were investigated. These outcomes are relevant to the proposed antibiotic study because they show that mitoribosome profiling can resolve codon-specific stalling, queuing, and initiation events, which are the same readout classes the plan uses to distinguish initiation inhibitors from context-dependent elongation inhibitors.
[context]
The paper context includes cell lines, MTU1 knockout cells, a MELAS-associated mitochondrial tRNA mutation background, and mouse cardiac muscle. Mitoribosome isolation was performed by sucrose density gradient, and the methods section lists lysate preparation, library preparation for MitoIP-Ribo-Seq and MitoIP-Thor-Ribo-Seq/MitoIP-Thor-Disome-Seq, standard Ribo-Seq and RNA-Seq, and data analysis. The excerpt does not specify HEK293 cells, antibiotic concentrations, treatment durations, nuclease digestion parameters, or statistical thresholds, so those details cannot be transferred to the proposed method without additional information.
[end related work 1]




[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Li et al., 2022-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/940dd61674973323a0b004866a30c27061b164c0)
- venue: Genome Research
- citation count: 32
[method]
The relevant paper method is low-input RNase footprinting with data analyses for mitochondrial translation. Cells were harvested, treated with RNase, ribosome-protected RNA footprints were purified, and sequencing libraries were constructed by A-tailing and SMARTer oligobased template switching. The resulting reads were analyzed for cytosolic and mitochondrial translation, including mapping to mitochondrial open reading frames and evaluating footprint-level features such as phasing, footprint length, and coverage. This is directly analogous to the proposed mitoribosome profiling plan, which uses read phasing, footprint length distribution, and coverage across mitochondrial ORFs to assess nuclease digestion and mitoribosome-protected fragment quality.
[results]
The method enabled simultaneous quantification of cytosolic and mitochondrial translation from 1,000 to 50,000 cultured cells and detected mitochondrial translation at base resolution. It identified TUFM-dependent changes in mitochondrial translation, showed suppression of cytosolic translation when mitochondrial translation was impaired, and resolved translated noncanonical ORFs in primary immune cells. The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical phasing scores, footprint length distributions, coverage values, or statistical significance tests.
[context]
Experimental context included low-input cultured cells in the 1,000-50,000 cell range, snap-frozen tissues, and primary immune cells. Library construction used A-tailing and SMARTer template switching, and analyses targeted both cytosolic and mitochondrial transcripts, including mitochondrial ORFs and noncanonical ORFs. The provided excerpt does not specify RNase concentration, digestion time, sequencing platform, read length, mapping parameters, or statistical thresholds.
[end related work 1]

