[method]
The paper used sucrose gradient centrifugation as the fractionation step for mitoribosome profiling. Mitochondria were isolated, lysed, and loaded onto a sucrose gradient; after ultracentrifugation, fractions were collected and ribosome species were resolved by absorbance. Mitoribosomes were recovered from the 55S peak, separate from the 80S cytoplasmic ribosome peak. The 55S fractions were then processed for mitoribosome profiling: nuclease digestion to generate protected RNA fragments, RNA extraction, reverse transcription, sequencing, and mapping of footprints to the mitochondrial genome. This is the same fractionation logic proposed for antibiotic-treated HEK293 cells, where 55S mitoribosomes must be separated from 80S cytoplasmic ribosomes before sequencing MRPFs to identify antibiotic-induced stalling sites.
[results]
Using this fractionation/profiling workflow, the paper detected mitoribosome stalling at AGA and AGG stop codons in mtRF1-deficient cells. Stalling was reduced when wild-type mtRF1 was restored, but not when a GGQ-mutant mtRF1 was expressed, supporting an essential role for the GGQ motif in termination. The profiling also revealed gene-specific effects: COX1 and ND6 transcript levels and translation were altered in different ways, and OXPHOS protein levels and complex activities were changed in mtRF1 loss. The provided excerpt does not include numeric fold changes, p-values, footprint counts, or gradient resolution metrics, so the quantitative outcome is limited to the reported 55S versus 80S separation and the qualitative stalling/rescue comparisons.
[context]
The related paper experiments were performed in mtRF1 knockout and rescue cell lines and, for in vitro translation/termination assays, bovine liver mitochondria. The key experimental condition for the proposed method is the physical separation of 55S mitoribosomes from 80S cytoplasmic ribosomes by sucrose gradient before MRPF sequencing. The paper's perturbation is genetic loss of mtRF1 rather than the antibiotic panel in the proposed plan, so it supports the feasibility of mitoribosome isolation and profiling but does not directly test antibiotic mechanisms. Exact gradient composition, rotor speed, nuclease choice, and sequencing depth are not specified in the supplied excerpt and should be treated as unspecified for reproducibility.
[end related work 1]



