[method]
The paper used ribosome footprint (RFP) and mRNA sequencing in two yeast strains to quantify translation at codon resolution. RFP libraries were prepared, reads were mapped to a modified genome annotation, and transcription and translation levels were quantified. The closest method to the proposed approach was calculation of ribosome residence time (RRT) for all 61 sense codons, which uses RFP abundance relative to mRNA to infer codon- or amino-acid-specific ribosome occupancy. The paper also used GO enrichment and motif investigation to interpret enriched sequence contexts, and it identified 5' extensions and 3' read-through events to assess translation boundaries. This relates to the proposed mitoribosome profiling plan because both rely on ribosome-protected fragment abundance at codon or amino-acid positions to infer translation features, but the paper did not use HEK293 cells, mitoribosome-specific profiling, or context-dependent bacterial translation inhibitors.
[results]
The provided excerpt does not report numerical RRT values, fold changes, p-values, or specific stalling motifs. It reports that RFP/mRNA analyses revealed strain-specific differences in gene expression and translation, including divergence in PUF3-associated genes and genes involved in mitochondrial translation, and that the two strains had compatible mitochondrial and nuclear genomes. No antibiotic-induced -1/P-site/A-site context-dependent stalling was measured.
[context]
The experimental system was yeast strains with mitochondrial replacement, not HEK293 cells or antibiotic-treated mitoribosomes. Inputs were mRNA and RFP sequencing libraries; analyses included genome annotation modification, read mapping, transcription/translation quantification, RRT calculation for 61 sense codons, GO enrichment, motif analysis, and identification of 5' extensions and 3' read-through. Specific growth conditions, sequencing depth, statistical thresholds, and software versions are not provided in the supplied excerpt.
